  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale  \n   We'll use wavelet transforms to decompose the function into frequency components, then optimize through Bayesian optimization guided by analytical bounds. This approach:\n   - Uses wavelet transforms for efficient optimization in multiple resolutions\n   - Incorporates gradient information to guide the search\n   - Balances global exploration with local refinement using Bayesian optimization\n\n2. Mathematical specification  \n   - Variables: h(x) \u2208 [0,1], \u222bh(x)dx = 1, dx=2/n  \n   - Objective: Minimize C5 = max\u2096 \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx  \n   - Repair function: Ensure integral=1, 0\u2264h\u22641  \n   - Gradient calculation: Use adjoint method with FFT for \u2202C5/\u2202h[i]  \n   - Bayesian optimization with analytical bounds and gradient information\n\n3. Implementation procedure  \n   We'll implement:\n   ```python\n   from scipy.optimize import minimize\n   import numpy as np\n   import time\n   \n   def repair(h, n=None):\n       if n is None: n = len(h)\n       # Implementation of repair function\n       # ...\n\n   def eval(h):\n       # Returns C5 value\n       # Use np.correlate with mode='full', then scale by dx\n       # ...\n\n   def compute_gradient(h, alpha=1.0):\n       # Analytical gradient of -C5 (since we maximize)\n       # Uses wavelet + FFT approach\n       # ...\n\n   def bayesian_optimization(n_start=560, max_time=1100):\n       # Initialize logger\n       best_c = None\n       t0 = time.time()\n       elapsed = time.time() - t0\n       h = initial_h_values.copy()\n       best_h = h\n       best_c = eval(h)\n       best_t = elapsed\n\n       # Start with optimization with full gradient\n       n = n_start\n       # Calculate dx\n       dx = 2.0 / n\n       # Set up optimization parameters\n       alpha = 10.0\n       max_iter = 2000\n\n       # First call with analytical gradient and integral constraint\n       # Define bounds and constraints\n       bounds = [(0,1)]*len(h)\n\n       # Wrap for optimization\n       def obj(x):\n           return eval(x)\n       def grad(x):\n           return compute_gradient(x)\n\n       # Use L-BFGS-B which handles bounds\n       h_opt = minimize(obj, h, method='L-BFGS-B', \n                          bounds=bounds, \n                          jac=grad, \n                          options={'maxiter': max_iter, 'disp': False})\n\n       h = h_opt.x\n       c_val = eval(h)\n       # Repair to ensure constraints\n       h = repair(h)\n\n       # Update best if improved\n       # ...\n\n       # Track time and budget\n       # ...\n\n   However, due to length constraints, I'll implement this Bayesian optimization with wavelets to detect improved performance.\n\n   Specifically, we need:\n\n   ```python\n   from scipy.optimize import minimize\n   import numpy as np\n   from skopt import gp_minimize\n   from skopt.space import Real\n\n   def objective(x):\n       return eval(x.reshape(len(h)))\n\n   def minimize_C5():\n       # Define parameter space\n       dimensions = [Real(0,1, \"h_{}\".format(i)) for i in range(n)]\n       dimensions[0:len(h)//2] = [Real(0,1, \"h_{}\".format(i), bounds_on=False)]\n       dimensions[-len(h)//2:] = [Real(0,1, \"h_{}\".format(i), bounds_on=False)]\n\n       # Initial h values\n       y0 = objective(np.array(initial_h_values, dtype=float))\n       # Create initial space points\n       space = []\n       for _ in range(5):  # 5 initial points\n           h_sample = np.random.uniform(0,1,len(h))\n           h_sample = repair(h_sample)\n           space.append({\"x\": h_sample, \"y\": objective(h_sample)})\n\n       # Perform Bayesian optimization\n       gp_min = gp_minimize(objective, dimensions, n_calls=40,\n                            noise=1e-1, verbose=False, random_state=42)\n\n       best_h = gp_min.x\n       best_c5 = gp_min.fun\n\n       # Return best solution\n       return best_h, best_c5, len(best_h)\n   \n   ```\n\n   This approach uses Gaussian Process to build a surrogate model of the objective function, minimizing both the number of expensive evaluations and getting good results.\n\n4. Search choices and variations  \n   We'll use:\n   - 5-point random initial population for Bayesian optimization\n   - L-BFGS-B for local refinement\n   - Wavelet-based gradient approximation for analytical bounds\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness  \n   - Dominant cost: Bayesian optimization with up to 50 evaluations, each O(n\u00b2)\n   - Dynamically reduce function evaluations based on elapsed time\n   - Fallback to initial solution if time expires\n   - Repair solutions before each evaluation\n\n6. Validation and return contract  \n   - Ensure all returned h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n   - Verify \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n   - Re-evaluate the final solution before returning\n   - Format: (h_values, best_c5, n_points)\n\nThis approach provides a significantly different optimization methodology from the previous strategies, focusing on adaptive sampling and Bayesian optimization to find better solutions.",
